WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.836
Today we'll learn how to use

00:00:01.836 --> 00:00:04.000
object masking in Adobe Premiere.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:06.000
Select your clip in the timeline.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.550
All of Premiere's masking tools

00:00:07.550 --> 00:00:09.000
are available in the toolbar.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:10.867
Select the Object Mask tool,

00:00:10.867 --> 00:00:14.000
then move your cursor over the program monitor.

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:16.405
Premiere will automatically detect and

00:00:16.405 --> 00:00:19.000
highlight objects or people in the frame.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:20.908
Click on the object you want to

00:00:20.908 --> 00:00:23.000
isolate to create the object mask.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:24.500
The first time you use this feature,

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:26.000
Premiere will give you a helpful tip

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:27.472
about where your mask data

00:00:27.472 --> 00:00:29.000
is stored on your computer.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:31.779
When the plus icon is active, you can select

00:00:31.779 --> 00:00:33.611
additional objects to include

00:00:33.611 --> 00:00:35.000
them in the same mask.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:36.765
When the minus icon is active,

00:00:36.765 --> 00:00:38.882
you can click on highlighted objects

00:00:38.882 --> 00:00:41.000
to remove them from the object mask.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:43.752
If your subject is not automatically detected,

00:00:43.752 --> 00:00:45.726
you can draw a rough rectangle or

00:00:45.726 --> 00:00:48.000
lasso around it to add it to the mask.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:49.529
Make sure the plus icon is

00:00:49.529 --> 00:00:51.000
selected when doing this.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:53.294
Depending on your footage, changing the

00:00:53.294 --> 00:00:55.529
mask overlay color can make the object

00:00:55.529 --> 00:00:58.000
mask easier to see against the background.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:00.103
Tracking controls for the object mask are

00:01:00.103 --> 00:01:02.000
located in the Effect Controls panel.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:04.674
When tracking, it's best to start from a frame

00:01:04.674 --> 00:01:07.000
where the subject is completely visible.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:10.586
Once tracking is complete, you can refine the object

00:01:10.586 --> 00:01:13.966
mask using options such as feathering, expansion,

00:01:13.966 --> 00:01:17.000
opacity, or invert, depending on your needs.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:18.973
For this demo, we chose a clip where

00:01:18.973 --> 00:01:21.000
the subject disappears and reappears.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:23.000
The object masking tool may need

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:25.000
help following complex movement.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:27.708
Fine-tune your mask selection by moving the playhead

00:01:27.708 --> 00:01:30.000
to the moment where your mask is incomplete,

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:31.969
and use the plus icon to add the

00:01:31.969 --> 00:01:34.000
missing assets back to your mask.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:44.000
Track the mask again to solidify your changes.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:45.762
Keep in mind that object mask data is

00:01:45.762 --> 00:01:47.381
saved externally in a masks folder

00:01:47.381 --> 00:01:49.000
located next to your project file.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:50.925
If you move or share your project,

00:01:50.925 --> 00:01:53.019
be sure to include this folder so the

00:01:53.019 --> 00:01:55.000
object masking data remains intact.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:02:02.000
